#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5dc7bc1-7cee-51ed-a77a-e3c95bd45d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232838}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232838} :answer-field/id #uuid "95689d09-12c1-5c25-ad3c-3364276260a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "06ab12ca-1261-529b-b77e-4551b3fd2404" :question/math-academy-id "q-340948" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{-2{x}^{6}+5x}$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=-2{x}^{6}+5x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-12{x}^{5}+5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-12{x}^{5}+5) \\\\  & =(-12{x}^{5}+5){e}^{u} \\\\  & =(-12{x}^{5}+5){e}^{-2{x}^{6}+5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={((-12{x}^{5}+5){e}^{-2{x}^{6}+5x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =(-12(1)^{5}+5){e}^{-2(1)^{6}+5(1)} \\\\  & =-7{e}^{3} \\\\  & =-7{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eeafba33-9d2d-5c84-83d1-aba9c2f1d3a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232840}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232840} :answer-field/id #uuid "67d8ceb6-7fd7-562b-b684-7cdcaee0de6d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c2fc6b2-b088-54ff-90dc-24056bf60933" :question/math-academy-id "q-340955" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{-3x}$ at the point where $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{1}{3}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1/3} & ={(-3{e}^{-3x})|}_{x=1/3} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3(1/3)} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-1} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234086 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f33e1827-8673-5c93-87ec-142118255194" :question/math-academy-id "q-48003" :question/problem "Given that $y=-3{e}^{5-x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=-3{e}^{u},\\,u=5-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-3{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =3{e}^{u} \\\\  & =3{e}^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234314 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cc156fa4-1ae1-5983-b7ca-740de211698e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71881" :question/problem "Given that $y=2\\cdot {5}^{x+{x}^{3}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=2\\cdot {5}^{u},\\,u=x+{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=2\\cdot {5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}5,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1+3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}5\\cdot (1+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1+3{x}^{2})\\cdot 2\\cdot {5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}5 \\\\  & =(2+6{x}^{2}){5}^{x+{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{ln}5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f680649b-aad0-5c21-b454-2962d171cc58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234935}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234935} :answer-field/id #uuid "94bd198b-189e-5b43-a53b-df64554d3cd5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "778dd5ee-c1ff-5286-be7b-93fe7ada6aaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-128191" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}.$ Assume that $y\\ne 0$ and $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$y=-2($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$+K)^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2C}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}+2C}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+2C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+K} \\\\ y & =-2{({x}^{2}+K)}^{-1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=2C$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d913ad1f-de16-5603-834b-341786551f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2/({x}^{2}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234884}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234884} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3011aa0-ebf7-5b93-b9a2-848f373cb2d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "93ff4d9b-f05d-54cb-bfec-91c45b5200dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-128192" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$y(x)=\\,\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From part 6, we found the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2{({x}^{2}+K)}^{-1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=2$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =2 \\\\ -2{({1}^{2}+K)}^{-1} & =2 \\\\ 1+K & =-1 \\\\ K & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2({x}^{2}-2)^{-1}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}-2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "182d03f6-7007-5d30-9f41-468940bf1b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8t(2{t}^{2}+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234577} :answer-field/id #uuid "88d80e90-19de-5aa6-bcad-6a5bb1a4fd17" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f760aed-6b31-5953-b3f9-00dcac8b3038" :question/math-academy-id "q-132974" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's define a function $h(t):$\n\n$$\nh(t)=(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}\n$$\n\nTo differentiate this function, we use the chain rule. Let \n\n$$\ng(t)=2{t}^{2}+3,\\,h(g(t))=g(t)^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating our functions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =(2{t}^{2}+3)^{′}=4t, \\\\ {h}^{′}(g(t)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}] & ={h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={h}^{′}(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =8t(2{t}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "340d32ab-0292-55d6-ad2a-da14464f67e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234632} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6445949-abe5-548c-ab63-85eaeea6ef0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b2e5f110-a6b6-523d-8a9c-2c0d4e144aec" :question/math-academy-id "q-167329" :question/problem "Given that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=${{field-1}}${e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $y$ is a composite function:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,h=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4efd78ec-0961-543d-89f6-569515b073d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234669}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234669} :answer-field/id #uuid "a091f6bc-b989-5a26-a703-2d9db59566b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d250de2a-60d6-5a40-8247-2b544c01b955" :question/math-academy-id "q-167332" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\n$${{field-1}}$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "We combine the sum and the constant multiple rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+3(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{x}+24x-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+24\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+24\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d81f09a-0feb-5148-a473-414d681185ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234761}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234761} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9c18185-4f4a-5388-bff5-21e01260a961" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b4e678b8-dc07-54a9-88b9-306f52add57d" :question/math-academy-id "q-167783" :question/problem "Given that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,-$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $y$ is a composite function:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,h=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cf72517-3161-51c9-a14c-e5763bd0a6ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234788}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234788} :answer-field/id #uuid "d167882f-97cc-578a-888d-a466d8df8618" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6f4bfc51-e9ec-5f3d-bb23-fc7c6d021c8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-167786" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\n$${{field-1}}$+C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "We combine the sum and the constant multiple rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+3(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{x}+24x-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+24\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+24\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2ab1da2-a1c8-5cce-81e3-77b494aca80e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-0.03t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234849}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234849} :answer-field/id #uuid "105ae6ea-d079-52d2-9fc8-366d8f97f343" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39cc2ed4-7534-5355-9c68-129ad8345d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-168077" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation in part 1 for $T\\ne 25.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$T\\,=\\,25+K\\cdot \\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\,$where $K$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we separate variables and integrate both sides with respect to $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.03(T-25) \\\\ \\frac{1}{T-25}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.03 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{T-25}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int -0.03\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-25} & =-0.03\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.03t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a perfectly valid general solution. However, if we want to write $T=T(t)$ explicitly, then we can solve for $T.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.03t+C \\\\ |T-25| & ={e}^{-0.03t+C} \\\\ |T-25| & =K\\,{e}^{-0.03t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary positive constant.\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be both positive and negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =25+K\\,{e}^{-0.03t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b45fa11b-7aeb-5dbf-a888-002c910fdf5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25+3{e}^{-0.03t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234823}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234823} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba2c9880-594d-5761-a31f-7e56aaee7f89" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cdb03268-a0ae-53d3-84de-7af60bc110d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-168078" :question/problem "Let $\\widehat{T}(t)$ be a particular solution of the differential equation from the previous part that satisfies the condition $\\widehat{T}(0)=28.$ Find $\\widehat{T}(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\widehat{T}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that the general solution is\n\n$$\nT=25+K{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $T(0)=28$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & =25+K\\,{e}^{-0.03(0)} \\\\ 28 & =25+K\\,{e}^{0} \\\\ K & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the required particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{T}(t)=25+3{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2402a50b-0ada-5a95-bd1c-d60ffe3a7054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238371} {:answer/id #uuid "3113425a-30f8-5c8b-88b3-34e913ccf225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238481} {:answer/id #uuid "79eaa63a-7157-5816-ab3b-72d58acb2790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239106} {:answer/id #uuid "f7406111-8e32-5b08-aa41-0f61204a9031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240235} {:answer/id #uuid "fd71d029-d6be-50c5-9b70-7d320b3399c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240297}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240297} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1159eaa-cd25-57b9-b6f3-0999eae2bb5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1086559f-da78-5256-be5a-8d4d9777777a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104055" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\n$$ that satisfies $y(0)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =x+C \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =-x-C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-0-C \\\\ 0 & =0-C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "158257b0-40d3-5bb7-bac5-6e36056355d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y=2{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238234} {:answer/id #uuid "16004206-d689-5265-94c9-1376fc2a7419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238241} {:answer/id #uuid "41e7778c-2d5c-54b6-9c23-07562072de44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|+y=2{x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238628} {:answer/id #uuid "77b24ff7-d9d9-52ef-a8e8-e1a351aed83f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y={x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239088} {:answer/id #uuid "dd57c394-1382-5901-b867-e989f439cece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|+y={x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239988}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239088} :answer-field/id #uuid "30ba2846-d824-5e4c-aa9d-3131e60267bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cf490795-4587-5d21-a17e-b28b1511e0a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-104058" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\n$$ that satisfies $y(1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y & ={x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|-1 & ={1}^{2}+C \\\\ 0-1 & =1+C \\\\ C & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\n$$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ in implicit form is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y={x}^{2}-2\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c2258f7-1c89-5ddf-b3f5-be41b960a73f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1+x-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238450} {:answer/id #uuid "783d1f44-8f84-5b7c-88aa-9989c97b87f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2+2x-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239096} {:answer/id #uuid "96fd893a-6a0d-574c-96a1-9c7c9ecdd73f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2x+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239359} {:answer/id #uuid "c1af356d-96a3-5dbc-a694-254027046ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239753} {:answer/id #uuid "e73db678-16bb-58f9-861b-b9f4c455ff5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{1+x+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240090}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238450} :answer-field/id #uuid "288ef88e-ee76-5cb6-ba28-c9169ad97783" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5f5fc67b-8b37-59a0-97e4-36b78ca0108f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104059" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}\n$$ that satisfies $y(-1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =-\\frac{1}{x}+x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}-Cx}{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{1-{x}^{2}-Cx}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{1-{x}^{2}-Cx},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(-1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(-1) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{-1}{1-(-1)^{2}-C(-1)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{-1}{C} & =1 \\\\ C & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}\n$$ that satisfies the condition $y(-1)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{1+x-{x}^{2}}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "481424ef-2c74-5499-af3e-08e3dea9c7d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{4}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238682} {:answer/id #uuid "53af9c48-3c28-5879-bbf4-c5ba3f2cb694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238773} {:answer/id #uuid "5acef49e-76b6-5522-ba50-3922470f8f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238841} {:answer/id #uuid "b94a09be-43ba-5aaa-bb0c-be4b94983aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239666} {:answer/id #uuid "e2fc50d5-241e-5e44-b681-3a645594909e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240052}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240052} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ffbe1bf-6c6b-54f5-a9b6-580dcd3d52fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "04d08db5-75c0-5a9e-a376-151cc06be633" :question/math-academy-id "q-104064" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{3}$ that satisfies $y(2)=\\frac{1}{8}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{2}(3{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{4}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(2)=\\frac{1}{8}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{2}^{4}-{2}^{3}-C} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8-C} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ C & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{3}$ that satisfies the condition $y(2)=\\frac{1}{8}$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75be7c90-32ab-5c64-906d-1dd7954d2121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239071} {:answer/id #uuid "82693f98-0710-5635-895b-2a14d84f4e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239189} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7da46f-6bf6-5179-ad99-a6c9aa84ab9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8{x}^{2}-{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239852} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ba6483-6e8b-5e55-93bc-ce43b1b86246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+8{x}^{2}-{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240204} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c87060-4f09-5b9a-ba36-024d4f3b3691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239071} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c9d1e6a-7b24-50e3-9ef5-4dad3d944987" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cdddaa9b-4174-550d-a7b6-1b8e1a3f2b29" :question/math-academy-id "q-104065" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$ that satisfies $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={\\pi }^{2}.$ Assume that $0<x<\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+8x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x+8\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x+4{x}^{2}+{C}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the integral on the right-hand side of this equality, we use a substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+{C}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{C}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nwhere $C={C}_{1}+{C}_{2}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={\\pi }^{2}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})+4{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}+C & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{\\pi }^{2}+C & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\ C & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$ that satisfies the condition $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={\\pi }^{2}$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{x}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b0d02e3-ea76-54be-9962-5ec9138cf3cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5y(y+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241559} {:answer/id #uuid "5eed5d22-c0e1-5129-9594-f6dbaafa7287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5{y}^{2}(y-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242032} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea7d0ef-a47c-52ec-93ff-921385515791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(9-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242295} {:answer/id #uuid "9fac9354-1235-5fb0-9542-c17bebd4f0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242579} {:answer/id #uuid "e25bbabc-1c66-5c6b-a765-1c2b1cf083e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5({y}^{2}-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243190}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242579} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c886461-a38a-59f3-8cbe-79521a21c395" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "23774b05-7a15-54bf-bb21-810326ad31c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-106647" :question/problem "Given that $y>9$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-9).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and $a=9.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>9$ for all $t>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86320bd9-da59-5656-8ab3-124ddf9658b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176-22{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242355} {:answer/id #uuid "c4580dbb-6ecc-59b2-a1ed-2744eb17551c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176y-22{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242907} {:answer/id #uuid "c50a56a4-5bf6-5a94-ba37-c1be22f8e9ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=22{y}^{2}-176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242915} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e66061-8542-5ca6-96ff-02c8087db693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176-22y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242961} {:answer/id #uuid "fa3e0ae2-02fc-5068-ada2-e9ae87b0b863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176+22y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243385}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242961} :answer-field/id #uuid "e899a014-1dbf-514f-a651-a2c2945018b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5077cc12-6631-56e6-a777-59697d379c16" :question/math-academy-id "q-106651" :question/problem "Given that $y>8$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=176-22y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-22(y-8).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=22,$ and $a=8.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>8$ for all $t>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b80c3e-e59a-547b-a782-41612e4da31a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-67(T-145)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241227} {:answer/id #uuid "1ccf8a1b-29f1-5b0a-80ad-9958a78a6231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-145(T-67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241422} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5e0f28-4de0-5f1e-ae81-d2cf901b0128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-kT(T-67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241889} {:answer/id #uuid "c6be0d3b-ed68-5a2f-af1a-64dc02f7559b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242933} {:answer/id #uuid "e362934b-110b-510b-8990-c6bccbecf578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-145)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243207}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242933} :answer-field/id #uuid "93430b60-75e1-51bd-a136-498bba75fcbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3ba4e3cf-6728-56eb-8731-1748c4ffae5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106883" :question/problem "A turkey reaches ${145}^{∘}\\text{F}$ in the oven. After removing the turkey from the oven, the turkey's temperature $T(t)$ decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${67}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the turkey? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={67}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-67).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad9d8a6-7aba-56a6-afbc-91568d96c3cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-76k(T-200)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241412} {:answer/id #uuid "2b82751d-3119-5dfe-92bf-dd1ff296563d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-200)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241566} {:answer/id #uuid "d98d1bb8-6a1f-57a1-837b-46446db882ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-76)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243117} {:answer/id #uuid "db649853-ab75-536f-99a1-1fb71fae0e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-200(T-76)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243128} {:answer/id #uuid "e321b854-ed39-5316-9cb9-9d7725a4859a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=200k(T-76)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243204}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243117} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f9c0873-1b25-595b-825b-fb1a95161c1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9d9f1ee5-0c1f-52df-9e3a-c2e55b83891f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106934" :question/problem "In a restaurant kitchen, a pot of soup reaches a temperature of ${200}^{∘}\\text{F}.$ After turning off the stove, the temperature $T(t)$ of the soup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the kitchen temperature is ${76}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the pot temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={76}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-76).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8b5f534-9e32-5a0a-b2bc-2ba8e2c86a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242660} {:answer/id #uuid "b384362d-2277-54cd-9282-ce1d30347703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-90(T-19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242745} {:answer/id #uuid "c6193ec8-264d-5ffa-8793-75573063af90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-90k(T-19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242925} {:answer/id #uuid "d68d698b-8905-5477-8ba9-77a836e1555f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-19k(T-90)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243081} {:answer/id #uuid "fa680411-3657-5df5-b80b-ed27eaecce77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-90)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243386}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242660} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a02deb8-c057-587d-9e15-ea05b099a20b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4f165134-d2dc-524e-9e90-e4a6ac3b7c02" :question/math-academy-id "q-106935" :question/problem "A container of water at a temperature of ${90}^{∘}\\text{C}$ is placed in a room. The temperature $T(t)$ of the water decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${19}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the water temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={19}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-19).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c073a7d-d446-5c47-a82c-ea083d458a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(T-30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241572} {:answer/id #uuid "88391a53-7653-5428-bd68-74e5bfdde4e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-kT(T-30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242376} {:answer/id #uuid "a366829a-9610-524d-adb6-9faa478168dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(30-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242612} {:answer/id #uuid "a84300b7-b40c-55e6-8dd2-005494b5d423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T+30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242657} {:answer/id #uuid "aa620027-8710-5351-b4aa-bc6b10e6c92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242670}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242670} :answer-field/id #uuid "7068eb8d-31e2-586e-b359-12d7347118f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "431988a0-8981-5d4d-9c5f-dd7ae32d4277" :question/math-academy-id "q-106936" :question/problem "The rate at which a pot of hot oil cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${30}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the oil? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={30}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-30).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ec0e10-da72-5c68-8c2a-a1567403fea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-75k(T-450)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241378} {:answer/id #uuid "89da823e-61ae-5588-8b2b-ecce0fbfcaee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-450k(T-75)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242393} {:answer/id #uuid "b581ec27-9282-5462-83a5-db99c3def9b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-450)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242765} {:answer/id #uuid "b96a94b4-0759-595d-b5e9-8c08089e01e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-75)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242806} {:answer/id #uuid "d243e252-a064-5c78-96c1-fe946bd46182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T+75)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243034}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242806} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9ed1cc7-4ade-5c7d-a721-5e4187d31c3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6cd430a4-4af3-5fcc-b21d-c7663e55325d" :question/math-academy-id "q-106937" :question/problem "A hair straightener reaches a temperature of ${450}^{∘}\\text{F}.$ After turning it off, the temperature $T(t)$ of the straightener decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${75}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the straightener temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={75}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-75).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40e0dff5-bfb1-5212-94c6-b1dadc022a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{96}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241760} {:answer/id #uuid "45e54fee-1ace-542a-8f41-af48ff15ba39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{92}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241808} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9fc5e0-ba4e-56c2-8bd8-2c6ee24e4cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242007} {:answer/id #uuid "a245084d-1bb7-5412-bc76-5d4162c98715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{76}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242601} {:answer/id #uuid "d36cea6e-e345-5856-9738-8be126828c2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243047}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241760} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa52215b-9a2a-5e79-b263-c1fe67ce6a74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b60de53-dea5-5dd2-908e-421b529445e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-107118" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cooling iron can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.1(T-28)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the iron is ${140}^{∘}C,$ calculate its temperature after $5$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the cooling iron is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.1(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-0.1\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-\\int 0.1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-0.1t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-0.1t+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.1t} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-0.1t} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-0.1t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the iron was ${140}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=140,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}140 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 140 & =28+K \\\\ K & =112\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+112{e}^{-0.1t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the iron after $t=5$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(5)=28+112{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 5}≈{96}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04eda6ac-1a0c-52d3-accd-9e4287c8ed9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{198}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241219} {:answer/id #uuid "1769c61b-0297-5fb6-9734-e3f5580daf02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{223}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241380} {:answer/id #uuid "65c47c3d-f35e-5489-ade5-8d1e2e7968d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{194}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242096} {:answer/id #uuid "728037c7-4e08-5de7-8ff2-e11877f70cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{201}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242193} {:answer/id #uuid "dcfe89bb-415b-5c7b-84d6-7bacf9027ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{189}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243147}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242193} :answer-field/id #uuid "393a690d-1e4c-5074-9abc-2b65ddbc8354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f919d480-6159-52e5-bf01-76d804f5fa17" :question/math-academy-id "q-107120" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Fahrenheit, of a fresh-baked cake can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the cake is ${340}^{∘}F,$ calculate the temperature of the cake after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fresh-baked cake is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-0.09\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-\\int 0.09\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-70| & =-0.09t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-70>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-70 & ={e}^{-0.09t+C} \\\\ T-70 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T-70 & =K{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T & =70+K{e}^{-0.09t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the cake was ${340}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=340,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}340 & =70+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 340 & =70+K \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=70+270{e}^{-0.09t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the cake after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=70+270{e}^{-0.09\\cdot 8}≈{201}^{∘}\\text{F},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123a42f8-2a35-5617-ba7d-f1b4d2c746b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241335} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc08283-0067-51f0-81a0-558fa4cdcf8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242008} {:answer/id #uuid "8346bdeb-1005-53c8-8841-f50949f617dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242327} {:answer/id #uuid "9c820d27-170e-5c1f-81bb-f4a2542fecd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242546} {:answer/id #uuid "ad515dcc-0231-5912-ac0e-df7d88012a7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242696}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242327} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bbd68ea-f66a-5917-98d1-898732b44fd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39da46ca-e83e-580d-8039-34194e24ef5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107122" :question/problem "A thermometer is taken out of a room where the air temperature is ${72}^{∘}F$  and brought outside where the temperature is ${15}^{∘}F,$ and its reading begins to decrease. After $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}F.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${25}^{∘}F?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred outside to ${15}^{∘}F.$*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={15}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-15).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-15| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-15>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-15 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-15 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-15 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =15+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${72}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=72,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72 & =15+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 72 & =15+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=15+57{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(2)=54,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}54 & =15+57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ 39 & =57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\frac{39}{57} & ={e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{39}{57}\\right) & =-2k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{39}{57}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${25}^{∘}F.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =25 \\\\ 15+57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =25 \\\\ 57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =10 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{10}{57} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{10}{57}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{10}{57}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${25}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{10}{57}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{39}{57}\\right)}≈9\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ae70af-887b-5770-9ba5-23db38cd4801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{31}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241332} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbae164-802b-58ab-91b2-3faf9544773d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241579} {:answer/id #uuid "4231eca7-f91a-5287-acdd-0be0665d65cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{43}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241773} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d27d97-2c83-55f0-970f-d85dcbf228a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{29}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242597} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3d7b5b-1863-5704-89a4-3e2c90453ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{58}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242693}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241579} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec7d5808-b789-5047-9d21-ccfd0fbce57b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ff863d3-8088-512b-858b-b0b33302607f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107129" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a compressor of a refrigerator can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.12(T-21)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the compressor is ${65}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the compressor after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the compressor of a refrigerator is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.12(T-21).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-0.12\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-\\int 0.12\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-21| & =-0.12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-21>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-21 & ={e}^{-0.12t+C} \\\\ T-21 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.12t} \\\\ T-21 & =K{e}^{-0.12t} \\\\ T & =21+K{e}^{-0.12t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the compressor was ${65}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=65,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}65 & =21+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 65 & =21+K \\\\ K & =44\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=21+44{e}^{-0.12t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the compressor after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=21+44{e}^{-0.12\\cdot 8}≈{38}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27cec937-8f89-509c-89d8-cac2418dad31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241525} {:answer/id #uuid "48530683-52e3-5b2d-b80c-52c96ea80021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241833} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea096e1-e11d-5b2f-9a38-2f0713ebe775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242165} {:answer/id #uuid "afef1e77-e7bb-55d4-85fd-246b46e4f2ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242713} {:answer/id #uuid "eb52bd30-42b5-564a-b65a-5b27e7b61017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243264}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242713} :answer-field/id #uuid "beb44a20-d166-5fe6-a7bc-aa72ceec3cb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0ce97e32-c8a9-5155-bb70-6cb7385360f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-107291" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{3-6x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3-6x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
